{"agent":"Monster3-UX-output-caps-7267fe","data":{"by":"Monster3-UX-output-caps-7267fe","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":6,"detail":"The bullet “`--full` (MCP `full=true`) lifts every cap for that call” is unconditional, but the `allow_full` knob description below says `allow_full=false` makes `--full` still return the capped answer. In an org layer or local config where `allow_full` is set to `false`, a user reading this line will expect `ddflow next --full` (or the MCP equivalent `full=true`) to return the uncapped list, while the actual behavior is a capped list with an explanatory note. The bullet should qualify that `--full` lifts caps only when `allow_full` is true.","digest":"a6b387db9049804b","location":"docs/ddflow/output-caps.md:10","severity":"LOW","title":"docs/ddflow/output-caps.md:10"},{"chunk":8,"detail":"The changed assertion now requires the truncation message for a `--json status` invocation to contain `ddflow status --full`, which drops the `--json` flag. The command that produced `body` in this test is `run_cli(repo, \"--json\", \"status\")`, so the named next call is not an equivalent rerun: a JSON consumer following it gets non-JSON output. This violates [R-audit-ux], which requires refusals/truncations to name the next call; the next call must preserve the invoked format (e.g. `ddflow --json status --full` or `ddflow status --full --json`).","digest":"f42095acc828a6ec","location":"tests/test_status_bound.py:60","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"tests/test_status_bound.py:60"},{"chunk":6,"detail":"The bullet at line 10 states that `--full` “lifts every cap for that call,” but the knob table at line 23 states that when `allow_full=false`, `--full` still returns the capped answer. In an org-lock environment with `allow_full=false`, running `ddflow next --full` will not lift the cap, so the advertised behavior is false for that configuration, and the footer’s suggested “everything: `ddflow next --full`” next call would not actually produce full output.","digest":"7a715cc78295bd4c","location":"docs/ddflow/output-caps.md:10,23","severity":"LOW","title":"docs/ddflow/output-caps.md:10,23"},{"chunk":1,"detail":"The change ships default output caps with non-zero values, so enforcement is active by default for every top-level read command. The change’s own conformance clause in the intent states that “new enforcement ships warn or off.” Here the caps are instead on: a user must explicitly pass `--full` (and have `allow_full=true`) to get unfiltered output. To satisfy the stated constraint, the first shipment should default `max_rows` and `max_chars` to 0 (off) or add a `warn_only` default before enabling truncation.","digest":"4653753578f1026a","location":"ddflow/config_sections/output.py: OutputConfig.max_rows=50, OutputConfig.max_chars=30000","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/config_sections/output.py: OutputConfig.max_rows=50, OutputConfig.max_chars=30000"}],"diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"output_digest":"165f2bf2152f893f","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/UX-output-caps.rubber_duck.lan-deepseek-v4-pro.20261104T175859Z-a5e10908.jsonl","review_kind":"delta","reviewed":[1,2,6,8],"status":"PARTIAL","terminated_by":"SIGTERM","tree_sha":"fb6df22b8d55+clean"},"gate":"rubber_duck","reason":"the review was terminated by SIGTERM mid-round after 4 chunk(s) were reviewed (1, 2, 6, 8); those findings are checkpointed, the remaining chunks were not reviewed, which is not a pass"},"id":"e73ffc48d06d6b4ef288165c5","kind":"gate.partial","lamport":135835,"schema":1,"subject":"UX-output-caps","ts":"2026-11-04T18:06:53.330515Z"}
